---
import { sourceLink } from '../data/catalog';

interface Props { subject: string }
const { subject } = Astro.props;
const introduction = sourceLink(['tos', 'tos/tos_en.ui', 'TOS and GEM in the original UDO source']);
---
{subject === 'subsystems' && <>
  <p>Atari introduced TOS in 1985 with the ST and developed it further for the STE, Mega ST, TT and Falcon.
    Its programming interfaces are grouped by responsibility. GEMDOS provides file, memory and process services.
    BIOS handles basic device I/O and system vectors, and XBIOS provides extended hardware services.</p>
  <p>The graphical environment, GEM, uses AES for interaction with the user and VDI for drawing.
    Line-A provides low-level 68k graphics operations. The PMMU functions-handler covers 68k memory management,
    and XHDI defines a common interface to block storage drivers. For other TOS-compatible systems, see
    <a href="/os-variants/">OS variants and extensions</a>.</p>
</>}
{subject === 'aes' && <>
  <p>GEM, the Graphics Environment Manager, is the graphical interface through which users interact with TOS.
    Digital Research developed GEM for Intel-based PCs in 1984. The Atari ST brought it to a wider audience.
    Other versions included GEM on X for Unix and X/GEM for FlexOS.</p>
  <p>Application Environment Services (AES) organizes the user environment through applications, windows,
    menus, dialogs and events. The <a href="/subsystems/vdi/">Virtual Device Interface</a> provides the graphics
    used to draw that environment. Follow the <a href="/conventions/programming-guidelines/style-guidelines/">GEM style guidelines</a>
    when designing user-facing programs.</p>
</>}
{subject === 'vdi' && <>
  <p>The Virtual Device Interface (VDI) is GEM’s standardized graphics system. It gives programs a common
    vocabulary for drawing lines, filled shapes, text and raster images through device workstations.
    <a href="/subsystems/aes/">AES</a> manages the application environment and uses VDI to draw its interface.</p>
</>}
{['subsystems', 'aes', 'vdi'].includes(subject) &&
  <p class="revision-note">Adapted from <a href={introduction.href}>{introduction.label}</a>.</p>}
{subject === 'ptos' && <p>pTOS is an EmuTOS-derived operating system with native builds for 68k, ARM and x32.
  Its boot files depend on the target and build configuration. These sections cover executable handlers,
  external EmuDesk and EmuCON, fonts, and the startup ABI for dynamically linked ELF programs.</p>}
